WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.824
Uncertainty means incomplete knowledge about facts, future states, risks, or outcomes.

2
00:00:05.824 --> 00:00:07.663
It is not a personal failure.

3
00:00:07.663 --> 00:00:12.415
It is the normal condition under which most important decisions are made.

4
00:00:12.415 --> 00:00:15.403
Technical teams do not know every dependency.

5
00:00:15.403 --> 00:00:18.009
Leaders do not know every market change.

6
00:00:18.009 --> 00:00:21.074
Security teams do not know every attacker path.

7
00:00:21.074 --> 00:00:25.059
Educators do not know exactly how every learner will respond.

8
00:00:25.059 --> 00:00:28.584
Probability is a practical language for uncertainty.

9
00:00:28.584 --> 00:00:33.719
It lets people move beyond vague statements like maybe, probably, or unlikely.

10
00:00:33.719 --> 00:00:36.937
A forecast does not need to be perfect to be useful.

11
00:00:36.937 --> 00:00:42.915
Saying there is a thirty percent chance of a delay is different from saying delay is possible.

12
00:00:42.915 --> 00:00:47.666
The first statement can support planning, contingency, and comparison.

13
00:00:47.666 --> 00:00:50.425
The second may be too vague to guide action.

14
00:00:50.425 --> 00:00:54.257
Base rates are one of the simplest ways to improve judgment.

15
00:00:54.257 --> 00:01:00.924
A base rate is background information about how often something tends to happen in similar situations.

16
00:01:00.924 --> 00:01:06.288
Before assuming a project is unique, ask how often similar projects finish on time.

17
00:01:06.288 --> 00:01:11.959
Before assuming a control will be easy to deploy, ask how similar deployments have gone.

18
00:01:11.959 --> 00:01:18.396
Specific evidence matters, but base rates keep teams from treating every case as entirely special.

19
00:01:18.396 --> 00:01:20.695
Confidence ranges are also useful.

20
00:01:20.695 --> 00:01:31.654
Instead of pretending a cost estimate is a single precise number, a team might say the likely range is between two values, with major uncertainty around integration work.

21
00:01:31.654 --> 00:01:37.171
Scenario thinking helps by asking what the world looks like if assumptions are wrong.

22
00:01:37.171 --> 00:01:45.754
Bayesian updating, at a high level, means revising beliefs as new evidence arrives instead of defending the first forecast forever.

23
00:01:45.754 --> 00:01:49.050
The phrase I do not know should not end the analysis.

24
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.195
It should begin better analysis.

25
00:01:51.195 --> 00:01:52.421
What do we not know?

26
00:01:52.421 --> 00:01:54.261
Which unknowns matter most?

27
00:01:54.261 --> 00:01:56.943
What evidence could reduce uncertainty?

28
00:01:56.943 --> 00:01:59.548
Which assumptions should be monitored?

29
00:01:59.548 --> 00:02:01.388
The goal is not omniscience.

30
00:02:01.388 --> 00:02:06.905
The goal is to make uncertainty explicit enough that decisions can be more resilient.
